ntdll: shrink a low-fragmentation heap block in place

Windows 7 shrank every heap block in place, so HeapReAlloc and the CRT's
realloc returned the same pointer for a smaller size. Programs of that era
shrink a buffer and keep using the old pointer. Windows 10's
low-fragmentation heap moves a block whose new size falls in another size
class, which frees the old one under them; Wine copied that. A visual-novel
engine's UI library crashes this way on Windows 10 and later.

A low-fragmentation block now shrinks in place whatever its new size, and
stays in its bin. Growth still moves it. The HeapReAlloc contract allows
both outcomes, and other blocks already shrink in place.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/heap.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/heap.c
@@ -2177,11 +2177,11 @@
 
 static NTSTATUS heap_resize_block_lfh( struct block *block, ULONG flags, SIZE_T block_size, SIZE_T size, SIZE_T *old_size, void **ret )
 {
-    /* as native LFH does it with different block size: refuse to resize even though we could */
-    if (ROUND_SIZE( *old_size, BLOCK_ALIGN - 1) != ROUND_SIZE( size, BLOCK_ALIGN - 1)) return STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
-    if (size >= *old_size) return STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
+    /* As Windows 7 does, a block shrinks in place whatever its new size class,
+     * and stays in its bin; only growth moves it. */
+    if (size > *old_size) return STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
 
-    block_size = BLOCK_BIN_SIZE( BLOCK_SIZE_BIN( block_size ) );
+    block_size = block_get_size( block );
     block_set_flags( block, BLOCK_FLAG_USER_MASK & ~BLOCK_FLAG_USER_INFO, BLOCK_USER_FLAGS( flags ) );
     block->tail_size = block_size - sizeof(*block) - size;
     initialize_block( block, *old_size, size, flags );
